Subject: [PATCH] nested wayland backend: forward a picked host mode to the host
 through wlr-output-management

Quasar patch against kwin 6.7.4 (Fedora 43: kwin-6.7.4-1.fc43).
Applies on top of 0001-nested-backend-mode-ladder.patch,
0002-nested-backend-kscreen-scale.patch and
0003-nested-backend-host-scale-hint.patch.

PROBLEM
-------
0001 lists the host's mode SIZES in Display Settings and "applies" a pick by
letterboxing: the content is scaled into the area the host configured us at.
That can only ever show a smaller picture inside an unchanged host mode. It
cannot change the refresh rate, cannot go LARGER than the configured size, and
never moves the display.

In a Quasar console session that is the wrong answer. The host (Quasar's
compositor) drives a real monitor, advertises that monitor's real modes on
wl_output - each with its own refresh, e.g. 3840x2160 at 59.996 and at
119.879 Hz - and implements wlr-output-management-unstable-v1 (v4): one head,
its modes, and `apply` of a configuration that picks one. The host then moves
the monitor and re-configures every toplevel at the new size. The nested KWin
just has to ASK, and 0001 drops the refresh and never asks.

WHAT THIS PATCH DOES
--------------------
1. `wayland_display.{h,cpp}`: bind the host's `zwlr_output_manager_v1` (up to
   v4) and mirror its first head - modes (size + refresh in mHz), current mode
   and the configuration serial. `requestHostMode()` sends one configuration:

       create_configuration(serial) -> enable_head(head)
         -> set_mode(the head's mode of that size, refresh within 500 mHz)
         -> apply

   and reports `succeeded` / `failed` / `cancelled` through
   `hostModeRequestFinished()`. One request in flight at a time. The protocol
   XML is vendored under src/backends/wayland/protocols/ and generated with
   ecm_add_wayland_client_protocol (an OBJECT library, as src/wayland does), so
   the build needs no new dependency.

2. `wayland_output.cpp rebuildModes()`: under a host with wlr-output-management
   the mode list is the head's modes, each with ITS OWN refresh (the same size
   at 60 and 120 Hz is two entries), larger sizes included. The native mode
   runs at the host's current refresh. The extra standard rungs
   (KWIN_NESTED_EXTRA_MODES) stay, at the current refresh, for sizes the host
   does not advertise. Without the protocol the list is exactly 0001's.

3. `applyChanges()`: a USER pick (OutputConfiguration::Source::User, i.e.
   Display Settings / kscreen-doctor) of a HOST mode is forwarded and the output
   deliberately stays at its old mode - no letterbox. When the host's configure
   arrives at the picked size and its head reports the picked mode as current,
   the pick is satisfied: it IS the native mode, contentScale() is 1/scale() and
   the offset 0. The two arrive in either order; `settleHostPick()` checks on
   both. A same-size refresh change needs only the head's current mode.

   Fallback: `failed`, a second `cancelled` (the first is retried once with the
   fresh serial, which precedes it on the wire), or no move within 2 s ->
   letterbox the pick exactly as 0001 does. A pick larger than the host's area
   cannot be letterboxed and is dropped with a warning. If the host gets there
   after the 2 s anyway, the letterbox is dropped again.

   A pick of an extra rung, and any pick under a host without the protocol,
   take 0001's letterbox path unchanged.

4. A configure WITHOUT a pending pick (the node agent moved the display, a
   stream-resolution step) is handled as before: it becomes the native mode,
   and 0001's stickiness keeps a letterboxed pick only while it still fits.

5. KWin's OutputConfigurationStore re-applies its remembered mode
   (Source::System) on every configure and at startup. Such a re-apply of a
   HOST mode is never forwarded and is otherwise ignored unless it is the mode
   the host is already in: the host owns which of its modes is current, and
   KWin must not move the physical display on its own initiative. (The store
   remembers a user pick through `desiredMode`, so after the host moves, its
   re-apply names the new current mode and clears any letterbox.)

6. `framePresented()` compares the presentation rate with `m_refreshRate`, not
   with the current mode's refresh. Those were the same value before; now the
   mode carries the host's advertised refresh (59996 mHz) and presentation
   feedback its own rounding (59995), and comparing the two would rebuild the
   mode list on every frame.

Nothing changes against a host that lacks wlr-output-management: no bind, no
request, no log line, the 0001 ladder and letterbox.

KScreen's "keep this configuration?" revert works for free: the revert is
another user apply, so it forwards the previous mode back to the host.

UPSTREAMABILITY
---------------
The forwarding (points 1 and 3) is a self-contained "nested KWin asks its
parent for a mode" feature that upstream could take on its own merits, with
upstream's single-mode list in place of 0001's ladder. Point 6 is a latent
upstream assumption (mode refresh == presentation refresh) that only holds
while the backend invents the refresh itself.

diff --git a/src/backends/wayland/CMakeLists.txt b/src/backends/wayland/CMakeLists.txt
index 437abf8..20f1cd8 100644
--- a/src/backends/wayland/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/src/backends/wayland/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -9,4 +9,21 @@ target_sources(kwin PRIVATE
     wayland_qpainter_backend.cpp
 )
 
+# Quasar: client side of wlr-output-management, used to forward a mode picked in
+# Display Settings to the host compositor. The XML is vendored next to this file
+# (MIT-style licence in its header) rather than added as a build dependency.
+# An OBJECT library for the same reason src/wayland uses one: target_sources() of
+# a file generated outside the directory that created `kwin` does not resolve.
+find_package(WaylandScanner REQUIRED QUIET)
+add_library(QuasarWlrOutputManagement OBJECT)
+set_property(TARGET QuasarWlrOutputManagement PROPERTY POSITION_INDEPENDENT_CODE ON)
+target_link_libraries(QuasarWlrOutputManagement Wayland::Client)
+ecm_add_wayland_client_protocol(QuasarWlrOutputManagement
+    PROTOCOL ${CMAKE_CURRENT_SOURCE_DIR}/protocols/wlr-output-management-unstable-v1.xml
+    BASENAME wlr-output-management-unstable-v1
+    PRIVATE_CODE
+)
+target_link_libraries(kwin PRIVATE QuasarWlrOutputManagement)
+target_include_directories(kwin PRIVATE ${CMAKE_CURRENT_BINARY_DIR})
+
 target_link_libraries(kwin PRIVATE Plasma::KWaylandClient Wayland::Client gbm::gbm)
